337.statement:
Simulate a 10 × 10 text terminal. The cursor starts at row 0, column 0 in overwrite mode on a blank screen. Normal characters are written at the cursor, then the cursor moves right if it can. In insert mode, characters from the cursor to the end of the row first shift right by one and the last one is lost.

A `^` starts a control sequence:

- `^b` cursor to the start of its row; `^c` clear the screen, cursor stays; `^d`, `^u`, `^l`, `^r` move down, up, left, right if possible;
- `^e` erase from the cursor to the end of its row; `^h` cursor to row 0, column 0;
- `^i` insert mode, `^o` overwrite mode; `^^` writes a `^` as a normal character;
- `^` followed by two digits moves the cursor to that row and column.

## Input

Several tests. Each starts with `N`, followed by `N` lines whose characters are fed to the terminal in order (line breaks themselves are ignored). `N = 0` ends the input. Every test starts with a fresh terminal.

## Output

For test `k`, print `Case k`, then the screen framed with `+----------+` above and below and `|` on both sides of every row. Match the sample exactly.

10267.statement:
Simulate a simple paint program on an `M × N` image whose pixels hold capital letters as colours; `O` is white. Coordinates are `X` (column, 1 to `M`) and `Y` (row, 1 to `N`) from the top left.

- `I M N` new white image; `C` clear to white;
- `L X Y C` colour one pixel; `V X Y1 Y2 C` vertical segment; `H X1 X2 Y C` horizontal segment;
- `K X1 Y1 X2 Y2 C` filled rectangle;
- `F X Y C` fill the region of same coloured pixels connected by sides to `(X, Y)`;
- `S Name` print the image; `X` stop.

A line starting with any other letter is ignored.

## Input

One command per line.

## Output

For every `S Name`, print the name and then the image row by row.

## Notes

The two ends of a segment or rectangle can be given in either order.

10550.statement:
A combination lock's dial has 40 marks numbered 0 to 39, and each mark is 9 degrees apart. To open it starting from the current position:

- turn the dial clockwise 2 full turns;
- keep turning clockwise until the first number of the combination is reached;
- turn counter clockwise 1 full turn;
- keep turning counter clockwise until the second number is reached;
- turn clockwise until the third number is reached.

Find the total number of degrees the dial turns.

## Input

One case per line: the starting position followed by the three combination numbers, all between 0 and 39. Consecutive combination numbers are different. `0 0 0 0` ends the input.

## Output

For each case, print the total degrees.

11360.statement:
Apply a list of operations to an `N × N` matrix of digits:

- `row a b` swaps rows `a` and `b`;
- `col a b` swaps columns `a` and `b`;
- `inc` adds 1 to every cell modulo 10 (9 becomes 0);
- `dec` subtracts 1 from every cell modulo 10 (0 becomes 9);
- `transpose` turns rows into columns.

## Input

The first line is `T` (`T < 50`). Each case gives `N` (`N < 10`), then `N` lines of `N` digits written without spaces, then `M` (`M < 50`), then `M` operations, one per line. Row and column numbers are 1 based and different.

## Output

For case `k`, print `Case #k`, then the final matrix as `N` lines of digits without spaces, then an empty line (also after the last case).

12096.statement:
Simulate a computer whose only storage is a stack of sets. The elements of sets are themselves sets. The stack starts empty, and after every command the computer prints the size of the topmost set.

- `PUSH` pushes the empty set `{}`;
- `DUP` pushes a copy of the topmost set;
- `UNION` pops two sets and pushes their union;
- `INTERSECT` pops two sets and pushes their intersection;
- `ADD` pops the top set `A` and then the next set `B`, and pushes `B` with `A` added as an element.

## Input

The first line is `T` (`0 ≤ T ≤ 5`). Each test case starts with `N` (`0 ≤ N ≤ 2000`), followed by `N` commands, one per line. No command pops an empty stack.

## Output

For each command, print the size of the topmost set. After each test case print `***`.
